---
import { Card } from "@bundu/ui/ui/card";
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import { crateStatus, npmStatus } from "../lib/registry";
import doctrine from "../data/doctrine.json";

// Versions are read at build time, never typed in: the MCP server's from npm,
// the Roots crates' from crates.io (they move together, so the umbrella's
// version stands for all ten). scripts/check-facts.py checks the built page.
const [mcp, roots] = await Promise.all([npmStatus("@nyuchi/mzizi-mcp"), crateStatus("mzizi-roots")]);
const rootsAt = roots.state === "published" ? ` at ${roots.version}` : "";

/**
 * The map. One row per public repository in `mzizi-dev`, with what it holds and
 * what it serves. Private repositories are not listed, including the one that
 * operates the MCP server — the endpoint is public and is named, the source is
 * not.
 *
 * `state` is deliberately not a marketing word. "shipping code" means the
 * repository holds a working implementation; "serving" additionally means a
 * hostname resolves to it; "placeholder" means a README and nothing else.
 * `site`, when set, is the live hostname a repository serves.
 * These were checked with `dig` and the GitHub API, not assumed.
 */
const repos = [
  {
    name: "mzizi",
    role: "The language",
    state: "shipping code, not routed",
    body: "The Mzizi programming language, and Phase 0 of its research charter (v0.4, “Mzizi: a general-purpose programming language”): the language's design (the charter and the RFCs, RFC-0001 to RFC-0013, with 0005 reserved), LANGUAGE-TRACKER.md (what the language still has to build, against Python, Go, C++, TypeScript and Rust), and the toolchain that implements it: the mz compiler front end — lexer, recovering parser, resolver, agent diagnostic protocol and mz fix, content-addressed IR, mz build, which lowers a service to a local Rust + axum package or a program to a Rust package with no dependencies, mz run, which builds and runs a program, and mz harness, which prints the language harness — nine primitives, an example service and five example programs written in Mzizi itself, and the public half of the benchmark harness, including the kill-criterion driver. The kill-criterion run has not happened. Rust.",
    href: "/language",
    internal: true,
  },
  {
    name: "mzizi-registry",
    role: "The components: design system and registry",
    state: "shipping code; its files are what api.mzizi.dev serves",
    body: `The components built to support the language, as its UI layer: the canonical component registry for the Mzizi ecosystem, shadcn-compatible and installable with the shadcn CLI. Also the brand system, the design tokens, the DNA double helix the frontend architecture is organised around, and Mzizi Roots, the Rust components: ten crates on crates.io${rootsAt}, with mzizi-roots (UI) and mzizi-roots-server (server) as the umbrellas. It also holds the component contracts (contracts/): a versioned, machine-readable contract for each of the Mzizi Dashboard Standard's 31 app components in @bundu/ui. Its files are what api.mzizi.dev serves (through mzizi-api), and it holds no database. Roots are moving to Mzizi's own render(): seven components have it so far (registry #506 and #510 are merged), and Dioxus is still a dependency of some of the crates until that migration finishes. TypeScript and Rust.`,
  },
  {
    name: "mzizi-api",
    role: "The registry API (formerly mzizi-api-gateway)",
    state: "serving api.mzizi.dev today",
    site: "https://api.mzizi.dev/v1",
    body: "Serves api.mzizi.dev, in mzizi-dev/mzizi-api (formerly mzizi-api-gateway): the registry API as a Rust (workers-rs) Cloudflare Worker, served since 2026-11-17 with the same routes and responses. Every route answers from the registry's repository files, generated at a pinned registry commit and bundled into the Worker. A bot moves that pin to registry main (checked hourly, merged only when CI and strict parity against production pass; mzizi-api#16), so the commit changes without an edit here: every response's x-mzizi-source header names the one being served. There's no origin, no database and no Supabase. The original gateway replaced the registry's own Worker on 2026-09-29 with zero unexplained differences in a 1,359-request parity check. The Rust Worker, served since 2026-11-17, passed strict parity against the previous TypeScript Worker: 1669 of 1669 identical. The repository was mzizi-dev/mzizi-api-gateway until its rename to mzizi-api.",
  },
  {
    name: "mzizi-console",
    role: "The console",
    state: "serving app.mzizi.dev today",
    site: "https://app.mzizi.dev",
    body: "app.mzizi.dev, standalone: its scope is the registry, the components, the tokens, the architecture and developer tooling. Astro renders the chrome as static HTML. Today it loads live data in the browser and keeps no database of its own. Decided, not built (mzizi-console#34): one Rust Worker serves Astro's output on the server, sign-in moves to the server (WorkOS, with a session cookie), and any state it keeps goes in Cloudflare D1. Replaces an earlier console app: the doctrine is one UI framework, Astro, with Rust first and TypeScript second underneath.",
  },
  {
    name: "mzizi-site",
    role: "This site",
    state: "serving mzizi.dev today",
    site: "https://mzizi.dev",
    body: "The front door and the agent-facing surfaces — llms.txt and the MCP card. Astro, static output, deployed as a Cloudflare Worker with static assets, serving the mzizi.dev apex.",
  },
  {
    name: "mzizi-docs",
    role: "Documentation",
    state: "serving docs.mzizi.dev today",
    body: "The Mintlify documentation site, live at docs.mzizi.dev (HTTP 200, checked 2026-09-27). It is the one home of Mzizi's documentation — the language, the registry, design foundations, patterns, blocks and charts.",
    site: "https://docs.mzizi.dev",
  },
  {
    name: "mzizi-roadmap",
    role: "Roadmap",
    state: "placeholder",
    body: "The Mzizi roadmap. An empty repository with a one-line README at the time of writing; the real plan of record is CHARTER.md in the language repository.",
  },
];
---

<Site
  title="Ecosystem"
  description="Every public repository in the Mzizi ecosystem: the language, then the components, the API gateway, the console, the docs and this site that support it — with what each one actually holds today."
  lede="Seven public repositories. Four serve a live hostname; the rest are code that is built but not routed, or a placeholder. This page says which is which."
>
  <p>
    Mzizi is a programming language and a project of the{" "}
    <a href="https://www.bundu.org">Bundu Foundation</a>, built by{" "}
    <a href="https://www.nyuchi.com">Nyuchi</a>. Nyuchi also runs its cyber section: the
    console and active cyber testing. Everything listed here is public and Apache-2.0.
    There are private repositories in the org too — they are not listed, and
    neither is anything that is not yet real.
  </p>

  {
    repos.map((r) => (
      <Card className="flow my-6">
        <h2>
          {r.name} <span class="state state-wrap">— {r.role}</span>
        </h2>
        <p>{r.body}</p>
        <p class="state">
          State: {r.state} ·{" "}
          <a href={`https://github.com/mzizi-dev/${r.name}`}>source</a>
          {r.site && (
            <>
              {" · "}
              <a href={r.site}>{new URL(r.site).host}</a>
            </>
          )}
          {r.href && (
            <>
              {" · "}
              <a href={r.href}>more on this site</a>
            </>
          )}
        </p>
      </Card>
    ))
  }

  <h2>The Bundu ecosystem</h2>

  <p>
    Mzizi sits inside the Bundu ecosystem. Its umbrella is the Bundu Foundation;
    Bundu is Shona for wilderness. The ecosystem's own summary is: One ecosystem.
    One doctrine. One source of truth. Many interfaces. The ecosystem image and
    its story are on <a href="https://www.bundu.org/ecosystem">bundu.org/ecosystem</a>{" "}
    and in <a href="https://www.bundu.org/newsroom/the-bundu-ecosystem">the Bundu newsroom</a>.
  </p>

  <p>In one line: bundu is the wilderness, shamwari is the ranger, nyuchi is the bee, mukoko is the hive, Mzizi is the root everything grows from.</p>

  <h3>The four pillars</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Bundu Labs</strong>, research, knowledge, standards and frameworks.
      The research and standards arm of the Bundu Foundation. It investigates
      infrastructure gaps across Africa and emerging markets, and publishes the
      standards, frameworks and vocabulary the rest of the ecosystem builds on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nyuchi Africa</strong>, platform and infrastructure. The platform and
      infrastructure company of the ecosystem: the Nyuchi API, the console, and its
      own tools and products. It builds platforms others build on; it is not an
      outsourced IT department or a managed service provider.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mukoko</strong>, the consumer pillar. <em>Mukoko is a product of Nyuchi Africa.</em>{" "}
      Mukoko is a pillar of Bundu and a product of Nyuchi Africa, built and operated
      by Nyuchi: the hive and the consumer pillar, and the proof that what is
      researched reaches production and is used by people, and that the
      infrastructure works.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shamwari AI</strong>, community-facing AI and conversational
      experiences. Shamwari AI is the ranger in the wilderness, guiding and managing
      things. Shamwari is Shona for friend: an AI companion with open weights,
      grounded in Zimbabwean law and policy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>.cell</strong> is a Bundu Labs project that maps directly to SiafuDB;
    its repository is siafuDB/cell. Its spec (0.1.0) is a draft and PROPOSED. The
    register is at <a href="https://standards.bundu.org/cell/">standards.bundu.org/cell</a>.
    The Mukoko tokens are a proposed project and are OFF the current roadmap.
  </p>

  <h2>Doctrine: Ubuntu</h2>

  <p>
    Ubuntu is not a marketing claim; it is the specification. Its five pillars are
    the spheres of belonging and its five principles the values practised in them;
    seven engineering constraints make them testable. The text below is copied from
    the doctrine files in{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi-registry/tree/v4.44.0/content/doctrine">mzizi-registry</a>,
    in the registry's sort order, and is served by Mzizi through{" "}
    <code>get_doctrine</code>; its topic <code>engineering-constraints</code> (MCP 0.24.0 and
    later) returns the seven engineering constraints. The HTTP API has no constraints route yet; only{" "}
    <code>/api/v1/ubuntu/pillars</code> and <code>/principles</code> exist.
  </p>

  <h3>Five pillars</h3>
  <ul>
    {doctrine.pillars.map((i) => (
      <li><strong>{i.title}</strong>. {i.description}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>Five principles</h3>
  <ul>
    {doctrine.principles.map((i) => (
      <li><strong>{i.title}</strong>. {i.description}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>Seven engineering constraints</h3>
  <ul>
    {doctrine.constraints.map((i) => (
      <li><strong>{i.title}</strong>. {i.description}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <p>
    The Bundu floor is the constraint that sets the smallest sizes a screen may use:
    the touch and desktop hit regions, the body text, the contrast and the spacing
    layers. It is <a href="/bundu-floor">The Bundu floor</a>, set beside WCAG 2.2, Apple's
    Human Interface Guidelines, Google's Material 3 and Anthropic's brand guidelines,
    and the <a href="/spacing">spacing layers</a> it starts from. The registry release
    that carries these values is in progress.
  </p>

  <h2>The one live agent endpoint</h2>

  <p>
    <code>https://mcp.mzizi.dev/mcp</code> is an MCP server over Streamable HTTP,
    serving the component registry as whole documents, the skills and the docs. It
    exposes three tools: <code>mzizi_search</code>, <code>mzizi_execute</code> (which
    runs the operations, such as <code>get_component</code> and <code>fundi</code>),
    and <code>mzizi_feedback</code>. It is Rust first: where a component has a Mzizi
    Roots implementation, <code>get_component</code> leads with it (the crate, the{" "}
    <code>cargo add</code> line, the contract and the <code>.rs</code> source), then
    the pure Astro build where there is one, and the React build last. It is free with
    no sign-in, except the <code>fundi</code> operation and issue reports through{" "}
    <code>mzizi_feedback</code>, which act as your console user. That is the owner's
    decision of 2026-09-29, and it is live:{" "}
    {
      mcp.state === "published" ? (
        <>
          <code>mzizi-mcp</code> {mcp.version}, the latest on npm when this page was built,
        </>
      ) : (
        <>the server (npm could not be checked when this page was built)</>
      )
    }{" "}
    answers <code>initialize</code> and <code>tools/list</code> with no token, and only the{" "}
    <code>fundi</code> operation and issue reports ask for sign-in (checked 2026-11-17). Its source is not public, so it has no row
    above.
  </p>

  <p>
    Discover its tools with the standard <code>tools/list</code> request. Any
    list of tool names written on a web page — including this one — can go
    stale, and the server is the only thing that knows what it currently
    exposes.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>

  <p>
    For anything about Mzizi — the language, the components, this site or the
    docs — write to <a href="mailto:support@bundu.org">support@bundu.org</a>.
    Security reports go to{" "}
    <a href="mailto:security@nyuchi.com">security@nyuchi.com</a>, as{" "}
    <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a> says, including those
    about the console at <code>app.mzizi.dev</code>. Bugs and proposals are welcome
    as issues on the repository concerned.
  </p>

  <h2>Naming</h2>

  <p>
    The Mzizi wordmark is capitalized: <code>Mzizi</code>. Every other brand
    wordmark in this ecosystem is lowercase: <code>nyuchi</code>,{" "}
    <code>mukoko</code>, <code>shamwari</code> and <code>bundu</code>. Package names, hostnames and code identifiers keep
    their literal spelling. <em>Mzizi</em> is Swahili for root.
  </p>
</Site>
